(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the principles of axonometric projection and classify it into Isometric, Dimetric, and Trimetric projections, detailing their coordinate axes angles and scale foreshortening.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Foundation of Axonometric Projection:) Tj
T*
(   - Axonometric projection is a category of parallel orthographic projection in which an object) Tj
T*
(   is tilted relative to the projection plane so that three mutually perpendicular faces are) Tj
T*
(   visible simultaneously in a single pictorial view.) Tj
T*
(   - Unlike multi-view orthographic projection \(which requires 2 or 3 separate views\), axonometric) Tj
T*
(   projection conveys immediate 3D spatial depth, form, and proportion to non-technical observers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Classification Based on Angles and Scale Foreshortening:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Isometric Projection:) Tj
T*
(   * Principle: The three principal coordinate axes \(X, Y, Z\) make EQUAL ANGLES with the) Tj
T*
(   projection plane.) Tj
T*
(   * Projected Axes Angles: The axes project onto the drawing sheet equally inclined at 120) Tj
T*
(   degrees to each other.) Tj
T*
(   * Scale Ratio: All three axes suffer the EXACT SAME foreshortening ratio: Scale X = Scale Y =) Tj
T*
(   Scale Z ? 0.8165 \(sqrt\(2/3\)\).) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Dimetric Projection:) Tj
T*
(   * Principle: Two of the three coordinate axes make equal angles with the projection plane,) Tj
T*
(   while the third axis makes a different angle.) Tj
T*
(   * Projected Axes Angles: Typically two angles are equal \(e.g., two axes at 105? and one at) Tj
T*
(   150?, or angles to horizontal of 7? and 42?\).) Tj
T*
(   * Scale Ratio: Two axes share an identical scale factor, while the third axis has a different) Tj
T*
(   scale factor \(e.g., full scale on two axes, half scale on the third\).) Tj
T*
(   - 3. Trimetric Projection:) Tj
T*
(   * Principle: All three principal axes make completely different angles with the projection) Tj
T*
(   plane.) Tj
T*
(   * Projected Axes Angles: All three angles between axes are unequal \(e.g. 105?, 120?, 135?\).) Tj
T*
(   * Scale Ratio: All three axes have different scale foreshortening factors, requiring three) Tj
T*
(   separate scales to construct. Highest visual realism, but most labor-intensive.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the mathematical relationship for the Isometric Scale factor sqrt\(2/3\) ? 0.8165, and provide the graphical construction procedure to construct an isometric scale on a drawing sheet.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mathematical Derivation of Isometric Scale Factor:) Tj
T*
(   - Consider a cube resting with its body diagonal perpendicular to the vertical projection plane) Tj
T*
(   \(VP\).) Tj
T*
(   - In this position, the top face of the cube is an inclined square ABCD.) Tj
T*
(   - Let diagonal AC be horizontal \(parallel to VP\). Because AC is parallel to VP, its projected) Tj
T*
(   length on the drawing sheet is its TRUE LENGTH \(TL\):) Tj
T*
(   Length of true diagonal AC = s * sqrt\(2\) \(where s is true edge length\).) Tj
T*
(   - In the projection, the square ABCD appears as a rhombus AbCd where diagonal bd is vertical.) Tj
T*
(   - In right triangle O-A-B \(where O is the center of the rhombus and OA = AC / 2\):) Tj
T*
(   * For True Shape of Square: Angle OAB = 45 degrees.) Tj
T*
(   cos\(45?\) = OA / AB_true => OA = AB_true * cos\(45?\).) Tj
T*
(   * For Isometric Projection of Rhombus: The side Ab is inclined at 30 degrees to horizontal) Tj
T*
(   diagonal AC \(angle OAb = 30?\).) Tj
T*
(   cos\(30?\) = OA / Ab_iso => OA = Ab_iso * cos\(30?\).) Tj
T*
ET

