ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Four-Center Method for drawing isometric circles \(isocircles\) on all three isometric planes \(Top, Left, Right\), providing exact geometric construction steps.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Basis:) Tj
T*
(   - In orthographic projection, a circle inscribed inside a square touches the midpoints of the) Tj
T*
(   four sides.) Tj
T*
(   - In an isometric view, the square becomes an isometric rhombus with angles of 60 degrees and) Tj
T*
(   120 degrees.) Tj
T*
(   - The inscribed circle projects as a true mathematical ellipse.) Tj
T*
(   - The Four-Center Method approximates this ellipse with four tangent circular arcs drawn from) Tj
T*
(   four distinct geometric centers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Step-by-Step Construction for Isoplane Top \(Horizontal Plane\):) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Draw the isometric rhombus ABCD of side equal to circle diameter D:) Tj
T*
(   * Sides AB and CD inclined at 30? to horizontal; sides BC and DA inclined at 150?.) Tj
T*
(   * Obtuse angle vertices are at A and C \(120?\); acute angle vertices are at B and D \(60?\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Mark the exact midpoints of all four sides: 1 on AB, 2 on BC, 3 on CD, 4 on DA.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3 \(Locating Centers\):) Tj
T*
(   * From obtuse vertex A, draw straight lines to opposite midpoints 2 and 3.) Tj
T*
(   * From obtuse vertex C, draw straight lines to opposite midpoints 1 and 4.) Tj
T*
(   * Centers 1 & 2: Vertices A and C are the centers for the two large radius arcs!) Tj
T*
(   * Centers 3 & 4: The intersection points of line A-2 with C-1 \(point P\), and line A-3 with C-4) Tj
T*
(   \(point Q\) are the centers for the two small radius arcs!) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4 \(Drawing Arcs\):) Tj
T*
(   * With center A and radius R1 = A-2 = A-3, swing a large arc from 2 to 3.) Tj
T*
(   * With center C and radius R1 = C-1 = C-4, swing a large arc from 1 to 4.) Tj
T*
(   * With center P and radius R2 = P-1 = P-2, swing a small arc connecting 1 to 2.) Tj
T*
(   * With center Q and radius R2 = Q-3 = Q-4, swing a small arc connecting 3 to 4.) Tj
T*
(   - The four circular arcs blend tangentially, forming a smooth isometric ellipse!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Application to Isoplane Left and Right:) Tj
T*
(   - The exact same geometric logic applies to the vertical isoplanes:) Tj
T*
(   * Isoplane Left: Rhombus drawn using 90? and 150? axes.) Tj
T*
(   * Isoplane Right: Rhombus drawn using 90? and 30? axes.) Tj
T*
(   * Connect obtuse vertices to opposite midpoints to find the four arc centers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Box Method and Offset Method for drawing isometric views of objects containing non-isometric lines, illustrating with a truncated pyramid.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Nature of Non-Isometric Lines:) Tj
T*
(   - Non-isometric lines are edges that are not parallel to any of the three principal isometric) Tj
T*
(   axes \(e.g., sloping roof edges, chamfers, pyramid slant edges\).) Tj
T*
(   - These lines DO NOT undergo the standard 81.65% or 30? foreshortening; their lengths cannot be) Tj
T*
(   measured directly with an isometric scale.) Tj
T*
(   - They must be constructed by locating their end vertices using coordinate measurements along) Tj
T*
(   isometric lines.) Tj
T*
ET

