T*
(   2. The Box Method:) Tj
T*
(   - Procedure:) Tj
T*
(   * Step 1: Enclose the entire object inside an imaginary rectangular box \(prism\) whose faces are) Tj
T*
(   parallel to the principal orthographic planes.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 2: Measure the maximum overall dimensions: Length \(L\), Width \(W\), and Height \(H\).) Tj
T*
(   * Step 3: Draw the isometric view of this enveloping rectangular box using isometric axes at) Tj
T*
(   30? and 90?.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 4: Locate the feature vertices on the surfaces of this isometric box by transferring) Tj
T*
(   coordinate distances from the orthographic views along the box edges.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 5: Connect these plotted points with straight lines. These connecting lines represent) Tj
T*
(   the non-isometric edges in their true isometric orientation!) Tj
T*
(   * Step 6: Darken visible outlines and erase or lighten the construction box lines.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The Offset \(Coordinate\) Method:) Tj
T*
(   - Procedure:) Tj
T*
(   * Employed when an object has many irregular sloping faces or curved contours \(such as stepped) Tj
T*
(   wedges or pyramids\).) Tj
T*
(   * Establish a primary reference plane or datum line \(e.g. the base plane\).) Tj
T*
(   * For each critical point P on the object, measure its three orthogonal coordinates \(x, y, z\)) Tj
T*
(   from the reference datum.) Tj
T*
(   * In the isometric drawing, plot x along the 30? axis, y along the 150? axis, and z along the) Tj
T*
(   vertical 90? axis to pinpoint point P.) Tj
T*
(   * Connect successive plotted points to form the complete profile.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
