(>> Solution to Question 6:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the representation of a Sphere in Isometric Projection versus Isometric View, providing mathematical derivations for the projected diameter and center position.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mathematical Geometry of a Sphere in Isometric Projection:) Tj
T*
(   - A sphere is a perfectly symmetrical 3D solid bounded by a surface of constant radius R.) Tj
T*
(   - Because a sphere is completely omnidirectional, its silhouette when viewed from ANY angle in) Tj
T*
(   space \(including any axonometric direction\) is ALWAYS a TRUE CIRCLE!) Tj
T*
(   - Under parallel orthographic projection onto the isometric plane, the projecting rays touching) Tj
T*
(   the sphere form a tangent cylinder of radius R perpendicular to the projection plane.) Tj
T*
(   - The intersection of this cylinder with the isometric plane is a circle of RADIUS EQUAL TO THE) Tj
T*
(   TRUE RADIUS R OF THE SPHERE!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Sphere in True Isometric Projection \(Reduced Scale\):) Tj
T*
(   - Projected Contour: Drawn as a TRUE CIRCLE with radius equal to TRUE RADIUS R \(Diameter = True) Tj
T*
(   Diameter D\).) Tj
T*
(   - Center Position:) Tj
T*
(   * The physical center of the sphere is located at distance R above the supporting base plane.) Tj
T*
(   * In isometric projection, all vertical distances are reduced by the isometric scale factor) Tj
T*
(   \(0.8165\).) Tj
T*
(   * Therefore, the center point O of the sphere is plotted at an ISOMETRIC HEIGHT:) Tj
T*
(   h_center = 0.8165 * R above the center of the supporting base!) Tj
T*
