(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the ISODRAFT command and 2D isometric drafting settings in AutoCAD, detailing how cursor crosshairs, grid lines, and isoplanes are manipulated.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. ISODRAFT Drafting Mode in AutoCAD:) Tj
T*
(   - Function: Transforms the standard orthogonal 2D drafting environment into an axonometric) Tj
T*
(   isometric simulation without entering true 3D solid space.) Tj
T*
(   - Command: ISODRAFT [Enter]) Tj
T*
(   - Options: [Orthographic/isoplaneLeft/isoplaneTop/isoplaneRight]) Tj
T*
(   - Status Bar Icon: Clicking the small isometric drafting icon toggles mode ON and OFF.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Behavior of Cursor Crosshairs and Drafting Grid:) Tj
T*
(   - When ISODRAFT is enabled:) Tj
T*
(   * The crosshairs tilt to 30?, 90?, and 150?, perfectly aligning with the active isoplane.) Tj
T*
(   * The background drafting grid \(F7\) tilts into an isometric dot/line grid oriented at 30? and) Tj
T*
(   150?.) Tj
T*
(   * When Ortho mode \(F8\) is turned ON, cursor motion is restricted strictly to the two axis) Tj
T*
(   directions of the currently active isoplane!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Cycling Isoplanes \(F5 / Ctrl+E\):) Tj
T*
(   - Pressing F5 cycles through the three isoplanes sequentially:) Tj
T*
(   * Isoplane Left: Crosshairs align at 90? and 150?. Used for drawing left-facing vertical) Tj
T*
(   surfaces.) Tj
T*
(   * Isoplane Top: Crosshairs align at 30? and 150?. Used for drawing horizontal top surfaces.) Tj
T*
(   * Isoplane Right: Crosshairs align at 90? and 30?. Used for drawing right-facing vertical) Tj
T*
(   surfaces.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Drawing Isometric Circles:) Tj
T*
(   - Standard CIRCLE command cannot draw isometric ellipses.) Tj
T*
(   - While ISODRAFT is active: Command: ELLIPSE -> Type 'I' \(Isocircle\) [Enter] -> Specify center) Tj
T*
(   -> Specify radius.) Tj
T*
(   - AutoCAD automatically draws the ellipse matching the tilt of the currently active isoplane!) Tj
T*
(   Pressing F5 during the command dynamically rotates the ellipse between Left, Top, and Right) Tj
T*
(   orientations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
